edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050780 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186221414 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050780 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186221415 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050780 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186221416 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050780 #edb/ref 1031 "Shifted Systems of ODEs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050780 #edb/ref 1033 6380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050780 #edb/ref 1034 0.8159553655380197 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050780 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "660c3818-8c01-5108-b77f-70c734343bed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050967 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050967 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050967 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186166919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050967 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186166920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050967 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186166921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050967 #edb/ref 1031 "Phase Portraits for Linear Systems With Repeated Eigenvalues" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050967 #edb/ref 1033 3240 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050967 #edb/ref 1034 0.4094434008891352 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050967 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "764fe6dd-703b-527f-a798-104e24a81888" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221181 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21674" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221181 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+3y(t)-4x(t)+4 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)-2x(t)+{y}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the corresponding linearized system at the equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast=[2, \\ 0]^T?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221181 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "87b69a53-94f5-51e6-9c3a-e403abbf1fa3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221181 #edb/ref 1074 "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}' = \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x})\n$$\n\nwhere ![](images/d4/d422a2e5dc28d7f3163879aa16f212b3573e9f2eb20f6aa1dd28ce2e8b55466b.svg) and \n![](images/2d/2df6f3b3a0c55d34c93291a33fca3f3f33f61a7491beb4cc495540a0f5ab5ae6.svg)\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n![](images/a5/a59de210f69e55b9de475da491dc1de9f32ccad7253ab1e4ab70714b96210e6d.svg)\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+3y-4x+4)=2x-4,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+3y-4x+4)=3 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-2x+{y}^{2})=2x-2,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-2x+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n![](images/28/2832518b9ff9a0795b8b1f337a92fbd97b8b1628da52ee88a2a9c88d95c330bc.svg)\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ![](images/0e/0e9e15cf4c6eb8e07e42b3c32fffe458c230511e50e02650f8c9bf4226801b5a.svg) to get\n\n![](images/cf/cf167eaf02d0500310e31db9b3db546c3dcd1b395438bdf5daaeefec2af500ae.svg)\n\nFinally, near the equilibrium, the nonlinear system is approximated by\n\n![](images/39/39b57ee5a860cfd3b73c64bebf93a77a193339530ae6970f931972a2e345a1df.svg)\n\nwhere the deviation vector is\n\n![](images/3c/3c80deae8395322a7abb6e106ccf084428b93e00e9b8087f53aa0c8bb76c00e4.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221182 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21675" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221182 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the system of differential equations, determine whether the system is almost linear at the equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast= [0, \\: 0]^T.$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-{y}^{2}(t)+1-{e}^{x(t)} \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-{x}^{2}(t)+1-{e}^{y(t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221182 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0f4ae74c-67d0-5c0e-99db-e834253b3851" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221182 #edb/ref 1074 "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}' = \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x})\n$$\n\nwhere ![](images/d8/d80eb6b2beaebedf071ab77f752156bfd4b433f3762deefc008751fbf0fd24f4.svg) and \n![](images/a8/a8e250dcd16fe545e136b5b58697f8ebf9ee674c44d31bebd5d4e9b67eb61a96.svg)\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n![](images/5f/5f841f13bd8e85cbac3b84dc57a3e9edaeef97ddedfa271e6befba2c97131015.svg)\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{y}^{2}+1-{e}^{x})=-{e}^{x},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{y}^{2}+1-{e}^{x})=-2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{x}^{2}+1-{e}^{y})=-2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{x}^{2}+1-{e}^{y})=-{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n![](images/77/77748e3e4d751bc1e9f6f2f8979440e46dd820e0263b893993ee8fd1bcdae50c.svg)\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ![](images/9b/9b03c0103e4d9b884776d63daa94a28cf078ec8e84bdaaf183744b6d4a493d75.svg) to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J(0,0) & =|\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{0} & -2(0) \\\\ -2(0) & -{e}^{0}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)(-1)-(0)(0) \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian determinant is not zero, our system is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{almost linear}}$ at $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221183 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21676" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221183 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/86/8635a08be7b8fa85e73b8683e0f17b95f9c834116d9ac29267d2ee5e98002856.svg)\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the behavior of the system’s solutions near the equilibrium ![](images/dc/dc2fe4cdba6d5eab5c763479aa564b60cf7c5c4736d958e7341223c30a580aab.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221183 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fd2d2d61-53a6-51c1-811c-41393270ddba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221183 #edb/ref 1074 "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n![](