17592186222329 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221470 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186222330 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221470 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221470 #edb/ref 1045 "Linear Approximations Near Equilibria" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221470 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186222324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221470 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221470 #edb/ref 1050 1140.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221470 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "495fe43b-de66-5798-9669-4b55442f64d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222005 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186368117 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222005 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186368122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222005 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334954" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222005 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222005 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+{y}^{2}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)-{y}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the system of differential equations, select the correct options below.\n\nThe Jacobian determinant of the system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\text{ }0]^{T}$ is given as\n\n$$\nJ(0,0)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nAt this point, the system is \n{{field-2}}\nat ${x}^{∗}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222005 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0c191cb5-1a9c-5ce5-b394-bfa06fef9db1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222005 #edb/ref 1074 "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\nJ=|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nJ=|\\begin{aligned}2x & 2y \\\\ 2x & -2y\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J(0,0) & =|\\begin{aligned}2(0) & 2(0) \\\\ 2(0) & -2(0)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian determinant is zero, our system is $\\text{not almost linear}$ at ${x}^{∗}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222324 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186222325 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222324 #edb/ref 1061 591480 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222324 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6570665c-9c0e-5698-9c44-79ef60fe6401" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222324 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186222494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222325 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186222326 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222325 #edb/ref 1061 591481 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222325 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4ea90440-2d8e-5c27-b532-f6be5d45b3b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222325 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186221417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222326 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186222327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222326 #edb/ref 1061 591484 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222326 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c10be260-d12b-595c-b241-523d377180f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222326 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186222496 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222327 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186222328 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222327 #edb/ref 1061 591485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222327 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "264a8b35-ed46-5392-a8ec-e88ead83801a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222327 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186221418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222328 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186222329 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222328 #edb/ref 1061 591488 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222328 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "828feab6-cd8f-5d79-9356-a39ac111b298" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222328 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186222495 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222329 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186222330 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222329 #edb/ref 1061 591489 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222329 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "29c915bc-5c95-5594-b0cf-10edbe6f04a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222329 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186221419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222330 #edb/ref 1061 591492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222330 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d039d8e2-81e7-5160-bcfd-7765a694f63a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222330 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186222497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222494 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider an autonomous system written as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'=\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}),\n$$\n\nwhere ![](images/60/60be83e58a72728b03ebfa2de6999ec80f17ee29e3f2ab3060d8ca43bbf45de8.svg) and ![](images/98/98bb67f212fa8deee63da1b69ae93086b9eb56a3ec64d70ba36e6a94dff1527b.svg).\n\nThe system is called **almost linear** at an equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ if the Jacobian determinant at this point is nonzero there, i.e.\n\n$$\n\\det\\!\\bigl(\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast)\\bigr)\\neq 0.\n$$\n\nFor example, let\n![](images/e6/e640b7ca9ddc8252223ec70f011147a702cd25bca0ee8a20314693695c1bec40.svg)\n\nFirst, notice that $\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast)=\\mathbf{0},$ so $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is an equilibrium.\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y+{y}^{2})=1,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y+{y}^{2})=1+2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y)=1,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n![](images/93/93058061ca42daa7114b615bcb77a8f9ccbc9cdccec98559db3eb288a7208940.svg)\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ![](images/72/72feeca197610c24c70b01f4c1075c75eebe89ae29f1aced83caeb252c275cce.svg) to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J(0,0) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1+2(0) \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian is $0,$ the system is *not* almost linear at $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222494 #edb/ref 1065 14492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222494 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fbbe8bd0-4cef-54d0-9803-a59eee727c6d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222494 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222495 #edb/ref 1064 "Again, suppose $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is an equilibrium of the nonlinear system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'=\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}),\n$$\n\nand assume the system is *almost linear* at $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$. If \n\n$$\nJ=\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast)\n$$\n\ndenotes the Jacobian matrix evaluated at the equilibrium, then the behavior of solutions near $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is determined by the eigenvalues of $J.$ \n\nThis information can be summarized in the following table.\n\n| **Eigenvalues of the Jacobian** | **Behavior** | **Stability** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| real and both positive | source / unstable node | unstable |\n| real and both negative | sink / stable node | asymptotically stable |\n| real and opposite signs | saddle | unstable |\n| complex with positive real part | spiral source | unstable |\n| complex with negative real part | spiral sink | asymptotically stable |\n\nLet's now consider a concrete example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222495 #edb/ref 1065 14493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222495 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a8d4acde-0ca3-57a4-9994-a551a9d0094b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222495 #edb/ref 1067 "Stability and Classification of Isolated Critical Points" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222496 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose the system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'=\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}),\n$$\n\nis almost linear at an equilibrium $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast.$ In this case, the system's behavior near $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ can be approximated by a linear system.\n\nFirst, we define the deviation from equilibrium by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{x}^\\ast.\n$$\n\nSmall values of $\\mathbf{u}$ correspond to points close to the equilibrium.\n\nNext, assuming $\\mathbf{x} = [x, y]^T,$ we compute the Jacobian matrix\n![](images/7a/7a0ff677dae0e51e34aed63faadfcb31f277f060c03488fdc1828cf8a2a5a0ec.svg)\nand evaluate it at $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ to obtain the constant matrix $J = \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast).$\n\nFinally, since $\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast)=\\mathbf{0},$ the nonlinear system can be approximated by the **linearized system**\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}'= J \\mathbf{u}.\n$$\n\nThis linear system captures the leading-order behavior of solutions close to the equilibrium.\n\nLet's see an example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222496 #edb/ref 1065 14494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222496 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9356e91f-f080-5827-a567-781aedac309a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222496 #edb/ref 1067 "Linear Approximations Near Equilibria" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222497 #edb/ref 1064 "In the classification above, we intentionally skipped one case: \n\nThe eigenvalues $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$ of the Jacobian at the equilibrium are purely imaginary. \n\nIn this situation, the corresponding linearized system has a *center* at the origin, meaning that solutions neither grow nor decay, but instead circulate around the equilibrium.\n\nFor the original nonlinear system, however, this case is *inconclusive*. While the linearized system predicts closed orbits, the nonlinear system *may* exhibit a stable center, an unstable spiral, or other nearby behavior.\n\nTherefore, when the Jacobian has purely imaginary eigenvalues, linearization alone is not sufficient to classify the equilibrium. In such cases, further analysis beyond linear approximation is required." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222497 #edb/ref 1065 14496 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222497 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5d168b25-7771-5e55-8be8-2eb16080b9b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222497 #edb/ref 1067 "A Note About Stable Centers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368108 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186368109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368108 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186368112 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368108 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334949" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368108 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368108 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y(t)) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={y}^{2}(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x(t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the system of differential equations, select the correct options below.\n\nThe Jacobian determinant of the system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[1,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ is given as\n\n$$\nJ(1,1)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nAt this point, the system is \n{{field-2}}\nat ${x}^{∗}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368108 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4b642f63-61e2-5bfd-b745-caa14baed777" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368108 #edb/ref 1074 "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y) \\\\ {y}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\nJ=|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y))=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y))=-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y) \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x))=-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x),\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x))=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nJ=|\\begin{aligned}2x & -\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y) \\\\ -\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x) & 2y\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J(1,1) & =|\\begin{aligned}2(1) & -\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi (1)) \\\\ -\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi (1)) & 2(1)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =4 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian determinant is not zero, our system is $\\text{almost linear}$ at ${x}^{∗}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368109 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186368110 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368109 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186368111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368109 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368109 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368109 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186368110 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368109 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "decc9c2a-219a-5a8c-88e2-22756060d4c6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368110 #edb/ref 1080 "almost linear" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368110 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368110 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2a6634ee-910f-55f8-9d60-5c8c995940b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368111 #edb/ref 1080 "not almost linear" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368111 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 1