e, we have the following:\n\n$$\nJ=|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y))=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y),\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y))=\\pi x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y) \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)-1)=-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x),\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)-1)=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nJ=|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y) & \\pi x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y) \\\\ -\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x) & 2y\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J(0,0) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) & \\pi (0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\ -\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) & 2(0)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian determinant is zero, our system is $\\text{not almost linear}$ at ${x}^{∗}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368126 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186368127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368126 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186368128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368126 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186368129 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368126 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186368130 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368126 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368126 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368126 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186368128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368126 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "7f025dfa-239c-52e6-a0d5-ea0f34a73cff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368127 #edb/ref 